WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.530
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:17.530
This is our second seminar
from the Refugee Program,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.270
for the fall 2021.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.110
Today we have the pleasure
of having Dr. Dany Bahar.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.160
He's an associate professor
at Brown University,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.570
Watson Institute for
International and Public Affairs,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.480
and a non-resident senior fellow

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.100
at the Brooking Institution Global Economy

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.990
and Development Program.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.520
He is an Israelian-Venezuelan economist,

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.840
with affiliation to the Growth Lab

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.570
at Harvard Center for
International Development,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.170
CESifo Group Munich,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:50.543
and IZA Institute of Labor Economics.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.720
Dr. Bahar research sits
at the intersection

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.840
of international economics
and economic development.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.190
In particular, he focuses on
the diffusion of technology

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.050
and knowledge within and across borders,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.320
as well as topics related
to structural transformation

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.460
and productivity dynamics.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.270
Lately, his focus has been
towards migrants and refugees

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.053
as an asset in the process
of economic development.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.310
Today, he will be speaking about
the Global Mobile Inventor.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.753
So Dany, the floor is yours.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:26.843
- Thank you, Teresa.

00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:30.180
And thanks everybody for
coming or joining on Zoom.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.780
It's great to be here.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.870
I just moved to New England,
and one of the parents,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.470
they told me, "You're close to Yale,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.510
and all those universities."

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.430
And now when I get the
chance to go to Yale,

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.560
I'm going on Zoom, but next time.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.133
And thank you for the invitation.

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.103
It's great to be here with you guys.

