00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.710
So, so it's going to be a little bit,

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.193
hopefully it stands out.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.090
And, but of course in the Q &amp; A, you know,

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.690
we can talk more about
other stuff we're doing

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.350
on the refugee aspect of it,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.003
but this is kind of, a
bit of a different crowd.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:13.763
And it's I think,

00:02:13.763 --> 00:02:16.570
a very exciting project that we started,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.403
we just started.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:18.236
And when I mean,

00:02:18.236 --> 00:02:22.147
we just started this just
two years ago before COVID

00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:24.520
and this is joined with Raj Choudhury

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:25.758
from Harvard Business School

00:02:25.758 --> 00:02:26.837
and Ernest Miguelez

00:02:26.837 --> 00:02:28.580
from the Université de Bordeaux,

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.330
who I think he's in the crowd.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.060
So, if there are questions on the chat,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.853
I think Ernest will be,

00:02:36.441 --> 00:02:38.070
he probably has answers that I don't.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:39.563
So he will be, also jump in.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.330
Anyway, let me start a little
bit with some, I guess, some

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.493
some motivation.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.510
And this quote we found

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.450
from I think a philosopher, Carlo Cipolla,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.640
who said, "Through the
ages, the main channel

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.010
for the diffusion of innovation
has been the migration

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:01.847
of people."

00:03:03.185 --> 00:03:05.110
And of course here,

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.760
one of the,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.703
one of the most notable
examples is this generation

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:17.453
of migrant scientists
who arrived to the US.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.100
And you could very much say,
you know, in a refugee status,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.853
the refugee status (indistinct)
really before World War 2.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.940
And, eh, you know, it's
attributed that these scientists

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.410
in their many different
fields really shaped

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.430
or lead shaped all science spectrum

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.173
in the US and in the world.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:42.780
And, you know, one,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.560
and there has been research done on this.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.393
I'm going to mention it,

00:03:45.393 --> 00:03:49.280
but the research by
Pedro Moser stands out.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.113
And, you know,

00:03:50.113 --> 00:03:54.830
it's clear that despite
the horrible circumstances

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.290
for which these scientists had to come,

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:58.853
had to flee their countries,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.650
the success of the US in this case,

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:09.010
in everything that has to do
with science and innovation

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.730
in the big part is due to this transfer

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:15.680
of brains and of people who settled here.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.470
And I think this paper goes a little bit

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.290
to speak about this.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.853
Let me start with one
example of what is it

00:04:20.853 --> 00:04:24.823
that we're after, an
anecdote I'll example.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.443
Meet Ismael Mena.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.740
Ismael Mena, he is, what I
think is a pioneer inventor,

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:40.283
he patented the first
ever patent in Chile,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.273
in the technology class A61B,

00:04:45.273 --> 00:04:50.273
which is a CPC
classification in the USPTO,

00:04:50.968 --> 00:04:52.480
the US Patents and Trade Office.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.850
So the first ever patent
that happened out of Chile

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.330
in that classification was made

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.157
by this gentleman called Ismael Mena.

00:05:02.157 --> 00:05:03.600
And this,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.490
the subclass A61B,

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.090
of course we don't know
exactly what it means,

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.990
but it's everything that has
to do with instruments in terms

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.260
of diagnostics and surgical and,

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.043
and medical instruments basically.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.440
After Israel Mena patented,
Ismael Mena, sorry,

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:26.470
patented this first patent
in Chile ever in 1996,

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.575
there were 17 more patents in that class,

00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:32.780
nine inventors in Chile,

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.900
and by the,

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.780
these are 17 patents by 19
other inventors in Chile

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.680
that happened after this first patent.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:46.320
So, you know, one question
I had, it's like, how come,

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.050
how did this guy do it?

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.733
Now, how did you get to a country?

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:53.123
Or like, you are,

00:05:53.123 --> 00:05:55.760
you're an inventor and you
start patenting something

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.860
that nobody else in the
country has done before,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.950
you know, it takes some I don't
know, courage, creativity,

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.750
how did you guys do it?

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:04.720
So I went and looked,

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.220
I mean this is a cherry picked example,

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:10.180
of course, but I went and
I looked into this patent.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:14.400
So this is the patent
that Ismael Mena filed

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.223
in 1996.

00:06:17.991 --> 00:06:19.470
It was filed in 1996.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.056
It was transferred in 1998

00:06:21.056 --> 00:06:23.690
We can talk about, we're
gonna talk about that later,

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:27.110
but you'll see, it's a
patent with a lot of people,

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.763
some of them in the US.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.050
But Ismael Mena is in
Chile, in Santiago, Chile.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.730
So of course you could
say, well, you know,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.990
he was patenting with other
people that knew about this

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.490
and all that, and that's all fair, but,

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:42.870
but stick with me for a
minute for the motivation

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:43.703
for this story.

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.480
But what's interesting here
is that when I go and I search

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.090
more of this person, Ismael Mena,

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.720
I find that another patent
in 1994, 2 years before,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.780
in the same subclass.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:58.723
So exactly in the same technologies,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:04.300
where Ismael Mena in Los Angeles, right.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:07.560
So, it turns out he was in the US,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.240
where maybe he did his
degree, where he was working.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.240
He patented, he learned
about this technology,

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.630
and then he moved to Chile,

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:16.800
which presumably is his home country.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.880
And then he patented more
in that same technology.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.700
So
our answer,

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.260
and this is what I'm going to
try to show you some evidence

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:28.810
in this talk or answers, how did he do it?

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.150
How did Ismael Mena was the first person

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:35.263
to ever patent a new technology for Chile,

00:07:36.413 --> 00:07:39.380
is because he was at
Global Mobile Inventor.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.760
- Dany, can I ask a question
for clarification here?

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.510
So the story,

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:46.509
I mean, one way to interpret
your story is that,

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:49.610
the inventor, wherever he goes,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.740
he invents and he takes
invention to a new place, right?

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.150
Another way to interpret
the story is that you move

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.140
to a new institutional
environment where you might,

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.880
you might not have necessarily a new idea,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:05.380
but in that environment,
those regulations,

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.160
you might want patent the same invention

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.660
or the same idea that you had, right,

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.010
that there's some value
to patenting in the US,

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.573
there's some separate value
to patenting in Chile as well.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.480
And which, which interpretation is,

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:21.343
is the one you would like us to.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.000
- I, if I understood it, right,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.650
I think it's more of a first one.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:29.040
Are you, by the second
one, are you saying?

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:29.873
- The second one is like,

00:08:29.873 --> 00:08:33.301
there's not necessarily a new innovation

00:08:33.301 --> 00:08:35.250
that's moving to a new place,

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.800
same innovation, given a
new regulatory environment,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.370
I might want to patent again.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.240
- I see. No, I think
it's more the first one,

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.780
because these are all patent in the USPTO,

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.950
in the same office, if
that answers your question.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.070
- [Ahmed] Oh, okay, okay.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:49.873
So it's not that he's
just replicating something

00:08:49.873 --> 00:08:52.880
in another country, but from Chile,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.630
he's now doing an additional patent

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.130
in the same patent office.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.610
So it's something above and beyond.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.490
- I see, so when we move
to the quantitative aspect

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.323
of your work,

00:09:03.323 --> 00:09:06.530
it will be holding constant,
the institutional environment,

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.500
like in terms of, like
say, the patent, okay.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.760
- Absolutely, yes, yes, exactly.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:11.593
Yes.

00:09:12.774 --> 00:09:15.050
That's a good, that's a great point.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:15.883
Okay, so, I mean,

00:09:15.883 --> 00:09:18.410
I'm making here a big
claim and I'm going to try

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:23.123
to show evidence, and to
see how far we can get.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.410
So, so what do we do in
this paper in particular?

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:30.820
So we really throw this idea

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.710
of what we call Global
Mobile Inventors or GMIs.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.423
And we're going to try to
answer questions such as,

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.323
how common are these GMIs,

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.220
how common are these GMIs,

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.000
and how do they differ from
other types of inventors?

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:47.840
Are they really,

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.227
do they really have very
different characteristics?

00:09:51.227 --> 00:09:54.313
Can GMI's explain the emergence
of new technology classes

00:09:54.313 --> 00:09:56.640
in the countries they go to?

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.310
And these are some, yes, exactly.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:00.430
Keeping constant as much

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:05.363
as we can including the
institutional environment.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.000
What else can we learn
about GMI's being pioneers

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.113
and in the process of diffusion
of knowledge and technology?

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.403
And here, I must be, I'm going
to be honest and upfront,

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:21.350
and please keep bringing
up these questions.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:24.060
You know, in this work in progress,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:27.943
we don't have yet a fancy
identification strategy.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.140
I'm not sure we really need it because we,

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:31.973
because in essence,

00:10:31.973 --> 00:10:33.140
I think a lot of what we're trying

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.140
to do is really descriptive
to try to show the evidence.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:37.970
So what, who are these people?

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.960
And I think we have some pretty
strong and robust patents

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.303
that makes it interesting.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.980
However, you know, I'm
going to talk about that,

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.700
we are still trying to see if there's room

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.520
for improvement when it
comes to identification

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:55.270
and you know, of course we
welcome ideas on that front.

00:10:56.333 --> 00:10:57.780
- [Sanjeev] I have a question.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.110
- [Dany] Yes.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:02.550
- [Sanjeev] So, does it
matter if the movement

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.853
of the GMI's are volitional
or non volitional.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:08.720
- [Dany] Are voluntary?

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:09.720
- [Sanjeev] Volitional, like, you know,

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:13.163
if they intend to move or
they were forced to move.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:18.200
- [Dany] I mean, it matters
in terms of endogeneity

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.953
of self selection, right-
- [Sanjeev] Yeah.

00:11:19.953 --> 00:11:22.290
- [Dany] You're going to.
- [Sanjeev] Yeah.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.140
- [Dany] Of course, it doesn't matter

00:11:26.075 --> 00:11:28.650
and I think that's going to
be our biggest challenge,

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:32.933
but here, you know, without
getting too much ahead of time,

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.800
there are kind of two
views of the world here.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:37.950
I mean, one of them,

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.470
let's imagine a firm wants to start being

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:47.010
in something, you know, that
they don't have the local,

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:51.340
the local know power, the local skills to,

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.450
and they will actually bring
somebody from the same firm,

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.130
from another location
and from another firm.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.240
That is a self-selection
process happening in process.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.500
The question is whether,

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.593
you know, that still means
that the counterfactual

00:12:04.593 --> 00:12:07.580
